thinking_content: '**Getting Started with Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration**


  Okay, so I''ve just read the research brief on integrating single-cell RNA-seq data to remove batch effects. I get the core problem: we need to smooth out the noise from different experimental runs while keeping the actual biological signals intact. The input data will be raw UMI counts (`adata.X`), and I can choose my own normalization approach. I''m thinking `normalize_total` followed by `log1p` should be a good starting point. The output has two options: either an integrated embedding (`adata.obsm[''X_emb'']`) or batch-corrected graph connectivities/distances (`adata.obsp[''connectivities'']` and `adata.obsp[''distances'']`). Crucially, I can''t use cell type labels for this part.


  The evaluation process is pretty straightforward, using 10 metrics with the average as the primary score. I''ve got some baseline numbers to beat: Combat Manual at 0.5895 and BBKNN Manual at 0.4138. The system''s general advice is to really think things through, but right now, I''m focused on getting familiar with the system.


  Following the system''s recommendations, I''m heading to the Private Memory Room to check my lineage. Since I just started this "Aletheia" lineage, there shouldn''t be anything there, but it''s good practice. Then I''ll return to the Research Counter. My first goal will be a basic submission to understand how the process works and how the evaluation judges my work.  I''ll need to figure out which of the available libraries (`scanpy`, `scikit-learn`, `numpy`, `scipy`, `JAX/Flax`, `pandas`, `anndata`) will be most useful for this.  A simple baseline is the priority here.

  '
